# Deploy — AWS EKS (Terraform + Helm)

This project was scaffolded with the **AWS EKS** IaC target. Infrastructure is
described with Terraform and the application is released with Helm. Every command
is scoped per environment via `--env`, which is **required** (no default) — run a
command without it to list the environments discovered under
`iac/terraform/environments/`. Add a new one with `dude iac new-env --env <name>`.
State lives in S3 + DynamoDB, so the same commands run locally and in CI.

!!! info "The IaC runner (Docker)"
    `dude iac *` runs terraform / kubectl / helm / k9s inside a pinned container
    built from `iac/runner/Dockerfile` (yours to edit) — so you only need
    **Docker** and the `aws` CLI installed, not the whole toolchain. Your
    `~/.aws` and `~/.kube` are mounted in, so profiles, SSO and the cluster
    context keep working. `dude iac login` stays on the host (it needs a browser
    for SSO); `dude iac shell --env <env>` drops you into the runner for ad-hoc
    work. Set `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host` to use your local tools instead.

## What gets provisioned

| Layer | Resource | Notes |
|-------|----------|-------|
| Network | VPC, public/private subnets, single NAT gateway | cost-conscious dev defaults |
| Cluster | EKS control plane + managed node group | Kubernetes **1.36** (standard support, no extended-support fees), `t3.medium` nodes |
| Registry | 2× ECR repositories | `{{projectName}}-backend`, `{{projectName}}-frontend` — **shared** across envs, created once by `dude iac bootstrap` |
| Ingress | AWS Load Balancer Controller | provisions an internet-facing ALB from the Helm `Ingress` |
| TLS / DNS *(optional)* | ACM certificate + external-dns | set `domain_name`/`route53_zone_name` to serve HTTPS on your domain |
{{#if withPostgres}}| Database | RDS PostgreSQL | managed; password generated by Terraform, `DATABASE_URL` wired into Helm automatically by `dude iac ship` |
{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}}| Cache/broker | Redis (in-cluster) | broker/result backend for Celery |
{{/if}}| Release | Helm chart `iac/helm/app` | backend + frontend{{#if withCelery}} + Celery worker{{/if}}{{#if withCeleryBeat}} + Celery Beat{{/if}} |
| State backend | S3 bucket (versioned) + DynamoDB lock table | created once by `dude iac bootstrap` |

## Deploy, in order

```bash
# 1. Authenticate to AWS for this environment (maps --env → an AWS profile)
dude iac login --env dev

# 2. One-time per project: create the shared backend (S3 state + DynamoDB lock)
#    and the shared ECR repos, then wire backend.hcl. The prefix must be globally
#    unique (e.g. your org slug). You do NOT repeat this for additional envs.
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <your-org> --env dev --yes

# 3. Initialise Terraform against the remote backend
dude iac init --env dev

# 4. Provision the infrastructure (VPC, EKS, ALB controller, …; ECR comes from bootstrap)
dude iac apply --env dev

# 5. Point kubectl at the new cluster
dude iac kubeconfig --env dev

# 6. Build, push and deploy the app in one step (build + push + helm upgrade --install).
#    The ECR registry, image tag, and (when a domain is set) the TLS cert + host
#    are wired automatically — no need to hand-edit values files.
dude iac ship --env dev
```

Check what's running:

```bash
dude iac status --env dev          # helm release + pods
dude iac output --env dev          # cluster name, ECR URLs, app domain…
```

## Add another environment (staging, prod, …)

Environments are independent infrastructure (their own VPC, cluster, database)
that **share one backend and one set of ECR repositories** — they differ only by
their Terraform state `key`. So adding `staging` does **not** require another
`bootstrap`; you scaffold the env folder, adjust its knobs, then init + apply.

```bash
# 1. Scaffold environments/staging/ by copying an existing env (default: dev).
#    Also copies its gitignored helm/app/values-<from>.yaml → values-staging.yaml.
dude iac new-env --env staging            # or --from prod to copy a different one

# 2. Review the new env's settings — set its domain and bump sizing for the tier.
#    Edit iac/terraform/environments/staging/terraform.tfvars:
#      domain_name = "staging.example.com"   # (optional) its own hostname
#      node_desired_size / db_instance_class / … to taste
#    …and iac/helm/app/values-staging.yaml (replicas, autoscaling, secrets, …).

# 3. Authenticate, initialise, and provision — same flow as the first env,
#    minus bootstrap (the backend + ECR already exist).
dude iac login   --env staging
dude iac init    --env staging
dude iac apply   --env staging
dude iac kubeconfig --env staging
dude iac ship    --env staging
```

> **Promoting an image.** Because the ECR repos are shared, you can deploy the
> exact image already built for one env to another instead of rebuilding:
> `dude iac deploy --env staging --tag <sha>` (use the tag from
> `dude iac status --env dev`).

## HTTPS on a custom domain *(optional)*

Set these in `iac/terraform/environments/dev/terraform.tfvars` (the Route53
hosted zone must already exist), then re-run `dude iac init && dude iac apply`:

```hcl
domain_name       = "dev.example.com"
route53_zone_name = "example.com"
```

Terraform issues a DNS-validated ACM certificate and runs external-dns to create
the record; `dude iac deploy`/`ship` then makes the ALB listen on 443 and
redirect HTTP→HTTPS automatically.

## Tear down

```bash
dude iac destroy --env staging        # destroy non-last envs first
dude iac destroy --env dev            # the last env also removes the shared backend + ECR
```

For each environment, `destroy` uninstalls the Helm release, deletes the
ALB / target groups / security groups the controller created, removes the Route53
records external-dns created for the env, and runs `terraform destroy`.

The **shared bootstrap** — the S3 state backend, the DynamoDB lock table, and the
ECR repositories — is torn down **only when no other environment still uses it**
(i.e. when you destroy the last one). Destroying a non-last environment never
touches another env's state or the shared registry. Pass `--keep-backend` to keep
the bootstrap even when destroying the last environment.

See `iac/README.md` for the full command reference and flags.
